WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.259
Making expensive, blockbuster films is an
exercise in high risk versus high reward.

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:09.780
Though most mega hit flicks have the potential
to bring in big bucks, they also have the

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.840
potential to lose a ton of money.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.189
So when a low-budget movie becomes a surprise
hit, studio execs can rest easy.

00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:20.630
We're talking those diamonds in the rough
that were made for less than $10 million but

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.410
still managed to gross loads of dough thanks
to positive critical reception, awards season

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.700
buzz, and innovative marketing and release
tactics.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.509
American Graffiti

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:35.130
George Lucas went on to make big box office
bucks with the Star Wars franchise, but before

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:40.410
he went to a galaxy far, far away, he went
to Modesto, California for his coming-of-age

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.540
drama American Graffiti.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.610
At the time, Lucas was a fairly unknown director
so he had a hard time getting financial support

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:48.860
for his project.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.920
Universal eventually agreed to make the movie
with just a $750,000 budget.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.220
However, they still questioned Lucas' vision,
asking him to hire a new editor and recut

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.399
the film after he turned in the finished product.

00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:05.780
The studio was worried about releasing the
film in theaters, but employees who had seen

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.790
and loved the film eventually convinced them.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:12.740
Despite the movie's troubles making it to
the big screen, it wound up being remarkably

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.510
well-received, earning five Oscar nominations
including Best Picture and Best Director for

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.510
Lucas.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:25.271
The film was immensely popular at the box
office as well, pulling in $115 million, making

00:01:25.271 --> 00:01:31.180
it the 15th highest-grossing PG-rated film
of all time when adjusted for inflation.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.650
Lost in Translation

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:37.530
The definitive fish-out-of-water dramedy,
Lost in Translation won writer-director Sofia

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:43.030
Coppola an Oscar for Best Original Screenplay
and earned three other nominations, including

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:44.180
Best Picture.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.430
But the Bill Murray-led movie, which explored
alienation and existential dread against a

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.270
bustling Tokyo backdrop, wasn't just an awards
season favorite.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:58.799
The $4 million flick earned $119.7 million
at the worldwide box office.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:01.110
"Can you keep a secret?

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.930
I'm trying to organize a prison break.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.509
I'm looking for, like, an accomplice."

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:10.709
Lost in Translation, which followed the complicated
relationship between Scarlett Johansson's

00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:16.510
aimless Yale graduate Charlotte and Murray's
aging movie star Bob Harris, first opened

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:22.459
on just 23 screens, earning a total of $925,087.

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:29.260
To put that into perspective, that's an average
$40,221 per theater the highest per theater

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.919
average for a limited release in 2003.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:33.950
Annabelle

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.999
Annabelle didn't buck the negative reviews
many horror movies face, but the $6.5 million

00:02:38.999 --> 00:02:41.909
creepy doll film made a killing at the box
office.

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:47.040
The movie very, very narrowly lost to David
Fincher's Gone Girl in its opening weekend,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.840
but still managed to pull in an impressive
$37.1 million.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.790
Annabelle's strong opening exceeded initial
$20 million expectations, at least in part

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.829
thanks to the movie's popularity on social
media.

00:02:59.829 --> 00:03:04.639
The movie also had the benefit of being spawned
from another popular, low-budget horror series:

00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:08.829
the Conjuring franchise, which came from horror
maven James Wan.

00:03:08.829 --> 00:03:14.019
The movie went on to make an impressive $257
million worldwide.

00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:15.540
The Blair Witch Project

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.290
The found-footage horror film The Blair Witch
Project wasn't just a cultural phenomenon

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.799
it was also a box office hit.

00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:28.420
The 1999 film about a group of student filmmakers
who hunt down a local urban legend was made

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:36.620
for just $60,000 but went on to make $246.8
million at the worldwide box office, including

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.540
$140.5 million domestic.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.879
This is even more impressive when considering
that the film was shot in just eight days,

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:46.189
with a partially improvised script.

00:03:46.189 --> 00:03:50.050
Like many of the movies on this list, The
Blair Witch Project benefited from an innovative

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:53.180
release schedule, rolling out slowly to spark
interest.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.479
The movie was screened at the Sundance Film
Festival without much fanfare, and audiences

00:03:57.479 --> 00:04:03.299
were left wondering if they'd just seen a
horror movie or the scariest documentary ever.

00:04:03.299 --> 00:04:07.479
Building on this excitement, filmmakers used
a viral marketing campaign one of the first

00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:13.309
to rely on the internet to hook viewers while
still maintaining the film's mysterious air.

00:04:13.309 --> 00:04:14.620
Get Out

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:19.040
Jordan Peele was already an established comedy
actor when he made his feature film directorial

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.150
debut with Get Out, a horror film which he
made for just $4.5 million.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.930
The movie wound up being a massive success,
earning $255 million worldwide.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.250
It was buoyed by near-perfect critical reviews
not to mention viewers' conversations surrounding

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:39.220
the movie, which pushed people to hit up their
local theaters to see what all the fuss was

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.220
about.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.570
Thanks to its innovative premise and some
smart marketing techniques, Get Out blew audiences

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:49.830
away and earned $33.3 million on its opening
weekend alone.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:54.911
Buzz for Get Out was so strong that the movie
managed to earn four Oscar nominations, despite

00:04:54.911 --> 00:04:59.260
the fact that it was released almost a full
year before nominations were announced and

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:03.510
the fact that the Academy has traditionally
overlooked horror movies.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:09.430
Peele's debut wound up being the most profitable
film of 2017, featuring a 630 percent return

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.570
on investment for production company Blumhouse.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:13.970
Juno

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:19.230
Penned by author/screenwriter Diablo Cody,
Juno earned four Oscar nominations, including

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:24.030
Best Picture, Best Director for Jason Reitman,
and Best Actress in a Leading Role for its

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:29.580
star Ellen Page, and a win for Best Original
Screenplay after it hit theaters in 2007.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.150
The film featured Page as Juno MacGuff, a
high schooler who debates her options during

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.650
a surprise pregnancy.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:41.420
The charming indie comedy was made on just
a $7.5 million budget, with star Jennifer

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:44.990
Garner even taking a pay cut to help the movie
keep costs down.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:49.650
But thanks to its witty, pitch-perfect writing
and strong appeal to teens and young women,

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:56.330
it ended up being a surprise box office success,
bringing in $231 million worldwide.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.330
Little Miss Sunshine

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.330
"Back up a minute…

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:01.080
You know what a loser is?

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.920
A real loser is somebody who is so afraid
of not winning, they don't even try."

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.560
The tragicomic 2006 indie flick Little Miss
Sunshine, which earned the love of critics

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.950
and fans alike, chronicled the trials and
tribulations of young Olive Hoover played

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:21.170
by Abigail Breslin who ropes her family into
a cross-country road trip so she can compete

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:24.010
in the Little Miss Sunshine beauty pageant.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:30.400
It also earned four Oscar nominations, including
Best Picture, which translated to two well-deserved

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.550
wins.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.450
Not too shabby for a movie that was made for
just $8 million.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:40.500
It ended up becoming a box office behemoth,
earning $100.5 million worldwide.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:47.710
"You're not nearly as stupid as you look."

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.090
Fox Searchlight, the studio that distributed
the film, clearly saw the box office potential

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:57.020
in Little Miss Sunshine right off the bat,
buying the film for $10.5 million after a

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:00.500
standing ovation at the first screening caused
a bidding war.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.840
Having that much buzz around the film right
out the gate got film enthusiasts excited,

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:09.050
while the quality of the movie led to a long
and successful box office run.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.180
My Big Fat Greek Wedding

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:16.180
My Big Fat Greek Wedding was just a few days
shy of spending a whole year in theaters,

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:21.080
and during that time, the $5 million romantic
comedy went from a low-budget film with few

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:27.960
big names to a massive money-maker, bringing
in $368.7 million worldwide.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.330
That box office success was very unique.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:38.030
The movie opened to just $597,362 from 108
theaters, but it continued to build up impressive

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:44.210
box office totals throughout its run, eventually
hitting the number two spot after 20 weeks.

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:47.419
The movie spent 17 consecutive weeks in the
top 10.

00:07:47.419 --> 00:07:51.710
The movie's success was truly unprecedented,
as most movies usually see a downward trend

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.030
after their opening weekends.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:55.020
Rocky

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.540
"He picked you, Rocky!

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.830
Rocky it's the chance of a lifetime."

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:05.889
Rocky may not be the top grossing film in
the long-running franchise at the box office,

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:12.370
but it's $117.2 million worldwide was especially
impressive when you consider that the film,

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:17.530
starring a then-unknown Sylvester Stallone,
had a measly $1 million budget.

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:22.420
In addition to its impressive box office haul,
the film won three Oscars, including Best

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:23.420
Picture.

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:27.460
Stallone also earned an Oscar nomination for
writing the script for the movie, but despite

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:32.089
its astronomical success, he actually had
a hard time getting it made.

00:08:32.089 --> 00:08:36.019
Stallone said that he went through "about
20 different incarnations" of the film before

00:08:36.019 --> 00:08:39.990
he landed on the finished project, but even
then it wasn't easy.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:45.069
Producers Irwin Winkler and Bob Chartoff had
to mortgage their homes and use their personal

00:08:45.069 --> 00:08:47.249
savings to finance the film.

00:08:47.249 --> 00:08:49.100
All that effort clearly paid off.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:54.519
The Rocky franchise holds the top four spots
for highest grossing boxing films, most recently

00:08:54.519 --> 00:08:59.490
taking over the box office with 2015's Creed,
which also was an awards season favorite,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:03.769
snagging Stallone an Oscar nomination for
Best Supporting Actor.

00:09:03.769 --> 00:09:04.769
Saw

00:09:04.769 --> 00:09:12.819
2004's torture flick Saw didn't just make
$103.9 million against a miniscule $1.2 million

00:09:12.819 --> 00:09:17.910
budget the movie also managed to launch a
lucrative horror franchise.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:22.170
But the original Saw was able to achieve its
initial box office heights by using strong

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:27.170
word-of-mouth to hold on to viewers throughout
its October and November box office run.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:31.290
Saw proved popular with young viewers, with
60 percent of the opening weekend audience

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.129
under 25 years old.

00:09:33.129 --> 00:09:37.550
That's likely thanks to the flick's young
director, then-newcomer, James Wan, who managed

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:41.570
to make the movie in just 18 days while keeping
the budget low.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:45.410
The franchise has continued to build on its
strong start, and although it has stumbled

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:52.730
at points, the eight films together have still
brought in a total of $976.3 million worldwide.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:59.089
The most recent release, 2017's Jigsaw, pulled
in $102.9 million worldwide against a $10

00:09:59.089 --> 00:10:03.819
million budget, proving just how impressive
Saw's initial feat was.

00:10:03.819 --> 00:10:04.819
Split

00:10:04.819 --> 00:10:10.170
M. Night Shyamalan went low-budget for his
2017 horror flick Split, making the James

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.309
McAvoy-starring thriller for only $9 million.

00:10:13.309 --> 00:10:16.959
This decision wound up working out well for
the twisty director, with the film pulling

00:10:16.959 --> 00:10:22.869
in $278.5 million worldwide and holding on
to the number one spot for its first three

00:10:22.869 --> 00:10:24.910
weekends at the domestic box office.

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:31.029
Split, an indirect sequel to Shyamalan's 2000
film Unbreakable, featured McAvoy as a man

00:10:31.029 --> 00:10:35.819
with what appears to be dissociative identity
disorder who kidnaps three teenage girls.

00:10:35.819 --> 00:10:41.750
The movie received positive reviews from critics,
earning a 76 percent on Rotten Tomatoes, and

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:45.170
was nominated for Best Thriller Film at the
Saturn Awards.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:49.730
Split became Shyamalan's fifth film to pass
$100 million at the domestic box office, proving

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:53.319
that the director still has the power to bring
in large audiences.

00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:57.459
However, Split's low budget made that figure
even more impressive.

00:10:57.459 --> 00:10:59.290
Paranormal Activity

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:00.790
Go ahead roll your eyes.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:02.490
You were expecting this one.

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:07.019
But whether you're tired of hearing about
it or not, Paranormal Activity's success story

00:11:07.019 --> 00:11:09.089
is one for the ages.

00:11:09.089 --> 00:11:16.820
It's not often a movie with a budget of $15,000
grosses $193.5 million bucks worldwide.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:21.720
The micro-budget horror flick was shot in
just seven days on a handheld video camera

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:26.940
by Israeli director Oren Peli, a video game
designer with no past film experience.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:31.529
After the movie terrified legendary director
Steven Spielberg, studio execs began to take

00:11:31.529 --> 00:11:32.529
notice.

00:11:32.529 --> 00:11:35.990
Paramount ultimately bought the film with
the intention to reshoot it with a higher

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:36.990
budget.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:41.240
However, when a test screening blew audiences
away, they decided to release Peli's version

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:43.959
of the film after drafting an alternate ending.

00:11:43.959 --> 00:11:49.390
Paranormal Activity's success was partly due
to positive reviews, sitting at an 83 percent

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:51.029
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:11:51.029 --> 00:11:55.399
Paramount also implemented an innovative marketing
strategy for the movie, putting it in limited

00:11:55.399 --> 00:12:00.660
release and letting it build buzz before setting
it into wide release the weekend before Halloween.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:05.699
In its first weekend in wide release, the
little ghost flick that could earned the number

00:12:05.699 --> 00:12:08.369
one spot with $21.1 million.

